In this test method, we first record the state of the array list before inserting the new element `x` at the `index`. After the insertion, we record the state again and calculate the results. We then use these results to validate our insertion. The preconditions and postconditions are observed (logged). We then make an assertion about the expected behavior, which is, after insertion, the index of `x` should be the same or larger than the index where we inserted the element. This is true for ArrayList, because inserting at a certain index `n` can potentially shift all elements after `n`.

Now, here are the results of running our Pex tests (it's pretty interesting):

```csharp
public void AddRangeContract(
    [PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, 
    ArrayList range, 
    int index)
{
    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_rangeCount = range.Count;
    int Old_index = index;

    arrList.AddRange(range);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_rangeCount = range.Count;

    // After adding a range, the new Count for 'arrList' is the old Count minus the old Count of 'range'
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - Old_rangeCount);

    // The new Count for 'range' should remain the same
    Debug.Assert(New_rangeCount == Old_rangeCount);

    // After adding, 'index' should not be higher than the old Count
    Debug.Assert(index <= arrList.Count);
}
```

In this test, we perform an AddRange operation in a contract test on an ArrayList. We do the same tracking using the assumptions and assertions before and after the operation. The main point of interest is the array list's count — after an AddRange operation, the new count should be the old count minus the old count of the range, and after an addition, the range's count should remain the same and the index added to doesn't exceed the array list's count. 

Overall, the use of the Pex framework has provided a detailed approach to test the ArrayList contract. The generated unit tests cover various scenarios and possible pathways in the ArrayList class. We used the framework to pre- and post-condition check as well as to cover edge-cases and assumptions, in turn generating comprehensive and robust tests.

But keep in mind, if you're planning to use Pex on a public library, it's a good practice to either use your own code or a modified version of the code with a dummy public interface that can be tested without violating the original code's purpose or functionality. -[_Source_](https://dzone.com/articles/test-driven-development-with-pester)
