In the first test case, we are inserting a value 'x' at a specified index in arrayList. The initial arrayList conditions are recorded to ensure the properties are maintained after the insert operation. After the insert operation, we check if the new arrayList contains 'x' at 'index' and if 'x' is in the list, then check New_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_index. The test case is designed to check that the behavior of 'Insert' method is as expected.

Please note for `PexObserve.ValueForViewing(...)`, you can view the values assigned to them by PEX in the result visualizer to debug and verify the test result. And for `Debug.Assert(...)`, you can setup breakpoints, and see the information right on these assertions.

The PexMethod under test is the ArrayList.Insert method (arrList.Insert(index, x)) that inserts an item to the ArrayList at a specified index. This test ensures that ArrayList.Insert behaves as expected, given the appropriate preconditions.

#### 5.3.3.8 [Contract Method Test](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-contract-test-methods-to-test-short-units-of-work?view=vs-2019)

A contract method is a test method, not a production function or a real method, it's designed to be used only in the test environment. It's a mock class in some languages and a dummy class in others. It only exists to test the behavior of the real class or method.

A contract test method is usually used to test other methodologies, it's for checking the outputs of a method as a whole, not a piece at a time. Contract test method is a subset or an abstract of what the production method really does, the contract test has no real purpose for the production code, it's just for testing the test.

Suppose we have a method add that we want to test, and the class looks like this:

```csharp
    public class MyClass
    {
        public int add(int a, int b)
        {
            return a + b;
        }
    }
```

A test could look like this:

```csharp
    [TestMethod]
    [HostType("Moles")]
    public void TestAddMethod()
    {
        MyClass t = new MyClass();

        // Arrange
        int a = 10;
        int b = 15;
        int expected = a + b;

        // Act
        int actual = t.add(a, b);

        // Assert
        Assert.AreEqual(expected, actual);
    }
```

This is a contract test, the method under test is the add method, and the contract is given by the expected value of the method, and the actual value is compared to the expected one.

#### 5.3.3.9 [Properties Test Method](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-property-test-methods?view=vs-2019)

Property testing involves testing the properties of the software under the test, as opposed to behavior driven development, where the focus is on the system's responses to a set of predefined inputs. This section examines the concept of property testing for unit testing.

A property is a requirement or condition that is true about a system or component under test (SUT). Here are some examples:

- In a sorting function, all elements are in the sorted state after the sort operation.
- In a database, all data retrieved by a query matches the query criteria.
- In a cryptographic algorithm, if two messages have the same content the same hash is calculated.

In property-based testing, inputs are generated randomly or based on a random distribution. The function under test is then expected to satisfy all the properties that it is supposed to. To implement property tests in .NET, a testing framework such as NUnit is often used. The test writer defines a set of properties and generates suitable input for these properties. When the system under test produces an output, the properties are checked to ascertain whether they are satisfied, and if not, the failed properties--------------------        }
    }
}

